WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:07.210
Not many people think about the history of
the mundane things, the things that have been

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:12.810
part of human history well ever since there
was a history, and beer is very much so one

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:13.920
of these things.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.359
I mean many people think about beer, or come
into contact with it but not many people really

00:00:18.359 --> 00:00:23.611
think about where it came from or how it evolved
into being the most consumed alcoholic beverage

00:00:23.611 --> 00:00:24.630
in the world.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:29.230
Now naturally when talking about a subject
such as beer, a staple of human civilization

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:34.289
far predating the written word, it is impossible
to talk about everything in one video.

00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:39.000
Therefore, this video will focus on just the
history and invention of ‘beer’ in the

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.510
early middle ages.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:44.800
If you're confused on how beer could be invented
in the early middle ages when I have already

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.260
pointed out that beer production occurred
far before then, well that’s because not

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.579
all ‘beer’ is created equal.

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:55.329
By that I mean the definition of the word
‘beer’ even today is varied, in fact the

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:56.329
definition of the word changed throughout
history.

00:00:56.329 --> 00:01:00.300
Some beverages that people today may call
beer would in the past be called ales, or

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.740
meads, which confusingly are also words that
have varied definitions and are sometimes

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.690
used as synonyms for beer.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.100
Lets create a very over-simplified diagram.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:15.110
Ale, Mead*, and Beer, are the most common
alcoholic brews, as in they need to be brewed,

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:19.890
which is a specific process of making alcohol
that separates these drinks from other historically

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.280
alcoholic beverages most notably wines or
ciders.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.280
So.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.970
Mead is an alcoholic beverage that ferments
honey sometimes with various fruits, spices,

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:30.250
grains, or hops.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:35.500
Mead mostly isn’t brewed but can be and
when it is it counts under the brewing category.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:40.250
Ale is an alcoholic beverage brewed with “malt”,
which is germinated grain, and most often

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:44.950
mixed with gruit, which is a mixture of multiple
herbs that varied widely depending on the

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:49.600
region it was made in, ale can also sometimes
be mixed with other things not just gruit

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.840
which makes its definition even more complicated.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:57.320
Lastly beer is an alcoholic beverage brewed
with “malt” and mixed with hops not gruit

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.020
or any kind of other herb or fruit like ale
can be.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.710
These definitions, however, aren’t complete
and today aren’t really used, for example

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:11.450
most Pale ales which are called ales do in
fact use hops not gruit while other drinks

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:17.099
just called ales still only use gruit, but
in the end we classify all Pale Ales or IPAs

00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:19.819
or just ales as beers.

00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:25.099
Even though beer should only be drinks that
use malt and hops, which a lot of ales don't,

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:29.450
they sometimes use other stuff on top of the
hops but as mentioned before they also use

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.170
gruit, fruits, or a mixture of various other
things.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:36.880
Not to mention, although rare, brewed meads
which use honey but also are sometimes mixed

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.010
with fruits, hops and malt are also called
beers.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:44.680
It seems like today the word beer is a catch
all for any kind of alcoholic brew.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:48.529
However, for most of history the word beer
wasn’t used this way and therefore when

00:02:48.529 --> 00:02:52.629
you look at historical finds labeled as beer
like one of the oldest records of brewing

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:57.629
from 7000BCE China where ‘malted’ rice
which is a grain, was found to be mixed with

00:02:57.629 --> 00:02:58.909
honey and fruits.

00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:04.110
It is hard to define if this was really beer
or if it was mead since it had honey or was

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:07.849
it just a type of ale and not beer since it
had no hops.

00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:12.779
But then again we call brewed beverages today
that don’t have hops beers, so why couldn’t

00:03:12.779 --> 00:03:14.069
this be a beer?

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:19.299
Anyway this entire complicated pedantic explanation
is to say that historically beer was only

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:24.719
used to describe an ale that was mixed with
hops not gruit or fruit or anything else,

00:03:24.719 --> 00:03:28.709
even though today we may use the word beer
to describe many more beverages than just

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:33.489
this, and today’s use of hops in beer is
a bit different than in the middle ages.

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:37.760
With that said, technically by it’s old
definition beer was created in the early middle

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.799
ages when people started brewing ales with
hops and anything before then, like Mesopotamian,

00:03:42.799 --> 00:03:49.111
Chinese, Egyption, etc. brewing was technically
just ales or brewed mead not beer, although

00:03:49.111 --> 00:03:53.709
I do agree that by modern definitions of the
word beer, those drinks could be called beers

00:03:53.709 --> 00:03:56.790
but we’ll ignore that for the sake of this
video.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:02.769
So who created the first beer?

00:04:02.769 --> 00:04:07.629
Well as with any firsts in history of very
common things like the wheel or tongs it is

00:04:07.629 --> 00:04:12.400
almost impossible to pinpoint a very first
instance of brewing beer.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.959
As the brewing of beer was very much a gradual
process with a lot of places like England

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:21.590
preferring to brew ales instead of beer all
the way until the 16th century.

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:26.140
With that said, by the time of the early middle
ages humans have been brewing mead and ale

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:27.320
for milenia.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:31.640
Using mostly gruit but also many other things
like fruits or honey to mix with the malted

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:35.440
grains like rice in the east and wheat and
barley in the west.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:41.510
Egyptians, Chinese, Mesopotemians, Greeks,
Romans, Celts, Slavs, Finno-Permic Tribes,

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:47.030
Scithians, Ilirians, Germanic tribes, etc.
all brewed some sorts of ales, although, of

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:52.030
course some people were more fond of them
than others, for example Romans viewed ale

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:57.370
as a peasant or even barbarian drink with
the higher classes drinking solely wine.

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:03.530
With almost every people group in Europe making
some sort of ale by mixing malt with various

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.970
things it wouldn’t be far fetched that at
some point someone somewhere happened to brew

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:10.910
malt with hops making the first beer.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:16.040
After all humans like to experiment with making
alcoholic beverages and hops was a known plant

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:21.710
in ancient Europe being used for mostly medicinal
purposes but also coloring or rope making,

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:26.520
so it wouldn’t be that weird to see brewed
beer early on, but since most people in ancient

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.890
Europe didn’t know how to write we can only
tell with certainty that Romans and Greeks

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:34.580
never actually mixed malt with hops not other
people groups.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:38.710
Although once writing did spread across Europe
there doesn’t seem to be any specific area

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:44.470
that extensively already used hops for brewing
so it is fair to say that before the 8th to

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:49.000
10th century any potential brewing with hops
would have been very limited with the practice

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.680
only growing in popularity during this time.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.421
What I find very interesting, however, is
that the first ever written mention of hop

00:05:56.421 --> 00:06:03.030
cultivation comes from Geisenfeld, Bavaria,
where in 736 a Western Slavic prisoner is

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:06.420
mentioned to be cultivating hops for some
reason.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:11.000
Now this could easily be hops cultivation
by a prisoner for medicinal or other purposes

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.800
but it is fun to speculate.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.670
It is also important to note that cultivation
of hops in the 8th century was quite rare,

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:21.860
outside Geisenfeld there were only a couple
of other mentions of it happening by the 9th

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:24.240
century mostly in France and Switzerland.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.490
This was because the way most people got their
hops for medicinal purposes was through gathering

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:32.900
of wild hops rather than cultivation, but
it wouldn't be far fetched to assume that

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:37.270
some cultivation of hops for medicinal and
other purposes did sprang up.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:41.580
Although with that said there is also no evidence
to suggest that this hops couldn’t have

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.510
been used for brewing as well.

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:47.780
In fact there are some archeological finds
that could support the brewing with hops as

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:52.170
early as the 7th century but more research
needs to be done into that.

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:56.420
There is also a theory that brewing with hops
comes from Eastern Europe specifically from

00:06:56.420 --> 00:07:01.010
the Balto-Finnic and Slavic tribes as their
word for hops “hmelj” “chmel” and

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:05.490
“humal” are from the same origin meaning
there must be some explicit reason why the

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:11.080
Slavic and Balto-Finnic tribes share the same
word for this specific unassuming plant.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:16.180
This is further supported by the fact that
an old Finnish oral myth that could possibly

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:21.540
be dated as far back as 3000 years ago mentions
brewing ale specifically with hops.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:25.920
However, this tale was only written down in
the 19th century when brewing with hops has

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.800
existed for about a 1000 years so it is very
plausible that this tale which was passed

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.870
down orally just changed the brewing section
of the tale from gruit to hops in the last

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:36.870
1000 years.

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:41.200
With all that said, the first mention of hops
directly associated with brewing was written

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:46.430
by Adalard of Corbie most likely in association
with the establishment of the new monastery

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:48.840
of Corvey in 822.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:53.270
In it hops was mentioned to be collected along
with firewood for the brewing process.

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:58.090
The entire writing was meant to be a sort
of guide on what jobs monks in monasteries

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:00.110
in the frankish kingdom should be doing.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:05.420
This means that this first mention of hops
in brewing in the 9th century only tells us

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:10.490
that brewing beer was already an established
practice in northern Frankish monasteries.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:15.210
Also from this mention and various similar
ones after it, it becomes clear that hops

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:19.930
wasn’t really cultivated for brewing during
this time, in fact it was gathered from the

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:23.050
wild just like when it was used for only medicinal
purposes.

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:27.620
It wasn’t until the 10th and 11th century
that more written records started to mention

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:31.740
hops being cultivated instead of just gathered
from the surrounding area.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:35.750
This cultivation of hops coincides with the
appearance of many more written records of

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.030
brewing ale with hops over gruit.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:42.530
However, as mentioned before our first written
record of brewing beer suggests it was already

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:46.880
an established practice and this is supported
by archeological records which show evidence

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:52.300
for brewing beer in the 8th or possibly as
already mentioned 7th century, with the practice

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:56.840
becoming more popular by the 9th and 10th
century and being quite widespread across

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:00.610
the entire HRE and northern France by the
11th century.

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:04.730
And yes despite some early written mentions
of beer brewing coming from northern France,

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:09.540
early beer seems to have been mostly just
a Holy Roman empire thing and actually within

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:12.860
the HRE it seems to have three main areas
of activity.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.800
Bavaria, Bohemia and the Low Countries.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:20.510
These three areas seem to have constantly
the most early archeological records about

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:22.740
beer brewing before the 10th century.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:27.380
However, the caveat here is that the low countries
don’t seem to have as many early written

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:30.060
records about beer brewing as Bavaria or Bohemia.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:35.230
Also the main varieties of hops all the way
until the 1970s were either from Bohemia or

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:36.860
Bavaria.

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:40.990
On top of that we see a large push back against
beer brewing instead of ale brewing in the

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:45.960
low countries around the 13th and 14th century
which doesn’t make sense if that area really

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:49.810
had beer brewing as early on as Bavaria and
Bohemia.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:54.210
What is most likely is that beer brewing probably
established itself in Bavaria, which has one

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:58.370
of the oldest written records about brewing
beer and Bohemia, which has one of the oldest

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:02.380
archeological records about brewing beer,
and from there the technology spread into

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:06.780
the Low countries where it was embraced much
more quickly than in the other areas of the

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:11.880
HRE therefore leaving a strong early archeological
mark despite the lack of written records.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:16.050
However, with that said I do admit more research
needs to be done into this area.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:20.210
So looking at Bavaria and Bohemia, and trying
to figure out which area did in fact start

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:24.840
brewing beer the first in around probably
the 8th century is impossible to tell.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:28.910
I personally like to believe that the Western
Slavs have discovered the superiority of beer

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:32.950
over ale and then showed it to the Bavarians
which where on the forefront of the Frankish

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:37.770
Slavic interactions, I mean remember our first
written record about cultivation of hops,

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:42.440
but this is purely a bias point on my part
and it’s just as likely that it was a Bavarian

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:46.690
invention picked up by the Western Slavs exactly
due to the fact that the Bavarians where on

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:49.380
the forefront of the Frankish Slavic interactions.

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:54.029
Nonetheless, the brewing of beer once it was
established in Bohemia and Bavaria did seem

00:10:54.029 --> 00:10:59.270
to spread quickly across the Holy Roman Empire,
most enthusiastically being embraced as mentioned

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:03.250
before by the Dutch and then northern German
trading cities.

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:07.180
This was mainly because hops preserved beer
for much longer.

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:12.370
Before hops ale with gruit had to be consumed
quite quickly or it would go bad, meaning

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:16.791
the brewing and consumption of ale usually
happened locally, but now with hops, beer

00:11:16.791 --> 00:11:20.450
could be traded over long distances as it
would survive the voyage.

00:11:20.450 --> 00:11:24.870
This explains why the trade oriented dutch
would be so keen on embracing beer so quickly

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:29.270
and thanks to them and the northern German
trading cities by the 14th century beer was

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:31.290
pretty much known all around Europe.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:36.130
However, this change from ale to beer didn’t
come without problems and there's truly far

00:11:36.130 --> 00:11:40.900
too many to mention here but the most notable
one that I already alluded to was the reluctance

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:43.510
of many local rulers to accept beer.

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:47.180
This was mainly because all across medieval
Europe there was a well established tax on

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:52.750
gruit which was used to brew ales before beer
was introduced and this tax was quite a sizable

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:57.070
part of the income to the upper class estates
and sometimes even the church.

00:11:57.070 --> 00:12:01.900
But now that people started to use hops, a
plant readily available in the wild, this

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:04.390
tax income started to disappear.

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:09.000
This meant that many rulers like for example
William the 3rd of Holland outright banned

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:14.530
the use of hops in brewing in 1321 but he
repealed this law just 2 years later presumably

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:16.270
due to public pressure.

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:21.400
Similar events of beer vs ale happened all
across Europe most notably in England in the

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.960
15th and 16th century.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:28.700
Lastly no early beer history would be complete
without mentioning monasteries.

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:33.520
Contrary to popular belief monasteries actually
accounted for little over all beer production

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:39.060
in history as most beer was brewed by independent
households themselves, or in urban areas in

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:41.540
some kind of a city established brewery.

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:45.600
Why we hear and talk so much about monasteries
when it comes to brewing is because they’re

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:49.680
the only ones who actually wrote about it
early on and they also represent a far more

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:54.770
stable and continues production of beer in
history which cannot be said about city breweries

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:56.230
let alone home brewing.

00:12:56.230 --> 00:13:00.970
This is why all the early written records
about beer brewing come from monasteries,

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:05.480
and also why these written records can’t
really be used as dating markers for brewing

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:10.040
ale with hops as these monasteries most likely
just picked up the practice of brewing with

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:11.770
hops from the local people.

00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:16.230
However, other than leaving us with written
records, monasteries were also very valuable

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:20.240
because it was probably through them that
the practice of brewing beer spread across

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:22.010
the Frankish Kingdoms.

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:27.830
I’ll conclude with a quote which I think
is quite apt during these times, it is from

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:33.930
an anthropologist M. Dougles who wrote about
alcohol including beer saying “They make

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:40.020
[and create] an intelligible, bearable world
which is much more how an ideal world should

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:52.490
be than the painful chaos threatening all
the time”

